{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And there was strife.</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 13:7): because the herdsmen of Lot were grazing their flocks unlawfully in the fields of others, and the herdsmen of Abram would reprove them for the theft, and they would say, 'The land was given to Abram, and he has no heir, so Lot will inherit it — and this is not theft.' But Scripture says, <b>'Now the Canaanite and the Perizzite were then dwelling in the land'</b>, and Abram had not yet acquired it. This is the midrash of our Rabbis (Genesis Rabbah 41:5).\n\nAnd I wonder: for the gift that was spoken to Abram was to his seed, as it is said above (Genesis 12:7), <b>'To your seed I will give this land'</b> — so how would Lot inherit him? Perhaps the herdsmen heard of the gift and were mistaken. And Scripture says that the land belongs to neither Lot nor Abram at present. And according to this, what it said at the beginning — that their possessions were great — is to say that because of the great possessions, the land could not bear them, and the herdsmen of Lot were compelled to bring their flocks into fields that have owners, and this is the cause of the strife.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, the strife was over pasture, for the land could not bear them, and when Abram's flocks were grazing in one pasture, Lot's herdsmen would come into their territory and graze there. Now Abram and Lot were sojourners and settlers in the land, and Abram feared lest the Canaanite and the Perizzite, dwellers in the land, hear of the weight of their flocks and drive them out or strike them with the sword and take their flocks and possessions, for the habitation of the land was now theirs, not Abram's. And this is the meaning of <b>'Now the Canaanite and the Perizzite'</b> — for it mentions that many peoples were dwelling in that land, and they and their flocks were without number, and the land could not bear them together with Abram and Lot. And from the word <b>'then'</b> (אָז), it seems to me that in those days the peoples in the land were tent-dwelling herdsmen, some of them gathering near a certain city and grazing there one year or two, and then moving from there to another region they had not previously grazed, and so they would do continually, like the custom of the sons of Kedar. And the Canaanite and the Perizzite were <b>then</b> in the land of the Negev, and the following year the Jebusite and the Amorite would come there."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:08.433941011Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}